course: books
lesson: 44
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter V, part 4: Dulce Domum (the carol, a magic supper, and a home to come back to)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: The field-mice sing an old carol about standing in the cold and snow, about Joseph and Mary finding a stable, and about the animals in the stable being the first to cry «Nowell!» As they finish, far-off church bells ring out above. «Very well sung, boys!» cries the Rat. «Come in and warm yourselves!»
explain: Then the Mole remembers — there's nothing to give them! «You leave all that to me,» says the Rat. He whispers to the field-mouse with the lantern, a coin chinks, and off he runs to the shops with a big basket. Meanwhile the others warm their chilly toes by the fire, and the Mole asks each one the names of his little brothers.
explain: The Rat warms up some ale with spices, as people did in those days, and the field-mice sip and cough and laugh. The Mole asks one of them to recite from last year's play, but the poor mouse is struck dumb with stage-fright. Just then the shopper is back, staggering under a full basket, and soon the table is covered with good things.
explain: Everyone eats, the field-mice tell the Mole all the local gossip, and the Rat quietly makes sure everyone has plenty. The guests go home with pockets full of treats for their little brothers and sisters. The Rat falls asleep at once. The Mole looks round his little room and understands: he will go back to the big world above, but it is good to know this small home is his and will always welcome him.
fact: The carol begins «Villagers all, this frosty tide, / Let your doors swing open wide» and each verse ends with «joy … in the morning». (Tide = time, season.)
fact: «Goodman Joseph toiled through the snow— / Saw the star o'er a stable low; / Mary she might not further go». The last verse asks: «Who were the first to cry Nowell? / Animals all, as it befell» (Nowell = Noel, a Christmas shout of joy; befell = happened).
fact: Then «from up above and far away … the sound of distant bells ringing a joyful and clangorous peal» (clangorous = loud and ringing).
fact: The Mole: «O, Ratty! … Whatever are we doing? We've nothing to give them!» The Rat: «You leave all that to me» — he is called «the masterful Rat» (masterful = taking charge).
fact: «Are there any shops open at this hour of the night?» — «Why, certainly, sir … At this time of the year our shops keep open to all sorts of hours.»
fact: The Mole hears bits: «Fresh, mind!—no, a pound of that will do—see you get Buggins's, for I won't have any other—no, only the best … no tinned stuff». Then «a chink of coin passing from paw to paw».
fact: The other field-mice sit on the settle, «their small legs swinging», and «toasted their chilblains till they tingled» (chilblains = itchy, sore spots on toes and fingers from cold).
fact: The Mole makes «each of them recite the names of his numerous brothers, who were too young … to go out a-carolling this year».
fact: The Rat finds the beer is «Old Burton»: «Now we shall be able to mull some ale!» (Mulled ale = beer warmed with spices.) The mice sip «and coughing and choking (for a little mulled ale goes a long way)». In the old days even children were given a little; today we know alcohol is harmful for children and they must not drink it.
fact: Last year's field-mouse play was about «a field-mouse who was captured at sea by a Barbary corsair (a pirate from North Africa), and made to row in a galley; and when he escaped and got home again, his lady-love had gone into a convent».
fact: The field-mouse asked to recite «giggled shyly … and remained absolutely tongue-tied» — «nothing could overcome his stage-fright».
fact: «Under the generalship of Rat, everybody was set to do something or to fetch something.» Mole sees «a lately barren board set thick with savoury comforts» (barren board = empty table).
fact: «The Rat said little or nothing, only taking care that each guest had what he wanted, and plenty of it, and that Mole had no trouble or anxiety about anything.»
fact: The guests leave «with their jacket pockets stuffed with remembrances for the small brothers and sisters at home». The Rat: «What a ripping little house this is!» (ripping = splendid). Sleep takes him «as a swathe of barley is folded into the arms of the reaping machine».
fact: The Mole sees «how plain and simple—how narrow, even—it all was; but clearly, too, how much it all meant to him». His old familiar things «now smilingly received him back, without rancour» (rancour = bitterness). He «knew he must return to the larger stage. But it was good to think he had this to come back to».
fact: Talk in this lesson: «craic» (Ireland) = fun, good times; «eejit» (Ireland) = idiot; «sweet as» (NZ) = great, no problem; «can or not» (Singapore) = is it possible?; «ate» = did it brilliantly.
exercise: What is the field-mice's song? | An old Christmas carol | A sea-shanty | A new song by the Rat | Ducks' Ditty
exercise: In the carol, who «toiled through the snow»? | Goodman Joseph | The three kings on their camels | The field-mice themselves | A shepherd on the hill
exercise: Who, says the carol, were the first to cry «Nowell»? | The animals in the stable | The angels in the sky above | The villagers | The children
exercise: What sound comes down the tunnel after the carol? | Distant bells ringing joyfully | The Otter calling | Wind | Thunder
exercise: What does the Rat say to the singers? | «Very well sung, boys! … come along in» | «Go away, it's far too late at night for singing» | «Sing it again» | «Who sent you?»
exercise: Why does the Mole despair again? | There's nothing to give the guests | The field-mice sing out of tune and the carol is too long | The fire goes out | They are too many
exercise: Who takes charge of the problem? | The Rat — «You leave all that to me» | The oldest field-mouse, who has a plan | The Mole | Nobody
exercise: Who is sent shopping? | The field-mouse with the lantern | The Mole himself | All the field-mice | The Rat himself
exercise: Why are the shops still open so late? | At this time of the year they keep all sorts of hours | They never close at all, not even on Sundays or at night | The Rat owns them | They aren't open
exercise: «See you get Buggins's, for I won't have any other.» What does this show? | The Rat is fussy about having the best | Buggins is a friend of the Mole's | Buggins's is the cheapest shop in town | The Rat is joking
exercise: What does the Rat NOT want? | Tinned stuff | Fresh food from the shop | Things from Buggins's | Only the very best
exercise: What do the field-mice on the settle do while they wait? | Toast their chilblains by the fire | Practise the carol again and again | Play skittles in the hall all evening | Curl up and sleep on the warm settle
exercise: «Chilblains» are… | Sore, itchy spots on toes from cold | Little wooden chairs for small children to sit on | A kind of hard ginger biscuit eaten at Christmas | Thick woollen gloves for frosty mornings
exercise: What does the Mole make each field-mouse tell him? | The names of his many brothers | How much money they collected from all the houses | A long riddle about the winter snow | A funny joke about the Rat's whiskers
exercise: Why didn't the little brothers come carol-singing? | Too young to be allowed this year | They had bad colds and coughs and had to stay in bed | They were lost | They can't sing
exercise: What does the Rat make with the «Old Burton»? | Mulled ale — beer warmed with spices | Hot sweet lemonade | Soup | Tea
exercise: What happens when the field-mice sip it? | They cough and choke and laugh | They fall asleep at once on the settle | They sing louder | Nothing
exercise: What was last year's field-mouse play about? | A field-mouse captured by a pirate and made to row a galley | A king who lost his golden crown in the snow and the wind | Toad's motor-car | The Wild Wood
exercise: Why can't the field-mouse recite a bit of the play? | Stage-fright — he is tongue-tied | He has forgotten every word of it | He has a sore throat from the cold | He doesn't want to
exercise: What stops all the talk of play-acting? | The shopper comes back with the basket | The Badger knocks loudly at the front door with his stick | The fire goes out | The Rat falls asleep
exercise: What does the Rat do during supper? | Says little, makes sure everyone has plenty | Talks all the time about the river and his boat | Eats the most | Sings
exercise: What do the field-mice give the Mole as they eat? | The local gossip, up to date | Presents wrapped up | Money | A song
exercise: What do the field-mice take home? | Treats for their little brothers and sisters | The rest of the beer bottles, and the sardines too | The glass ball | Nothing
exercise: How quickly does the Rat fall asleep? | At once | After a long talk with the Mole by the fire | He can't sleep at all | Only at dawn, when birds sing
exercise: What does the Mole understand at the end of the chapter? | He'll return to the big world, but it's good to have this home | He will stay at Mole End for ever and never go out into the sun again | He should sell his house | He doesn't need a home
exercise: How do the Mole's old things seem to greet him? | Smilingly, without rancour (without bitterness) | Angrily and sadly, for leaving them alone so long in the dust | Coldly | They don't
test: Oisín from Galway: «great craic, the Mole pays for the whole feast.» (craic = fun) Put it right. | The Rat pays — a coin chinks from his paw to the mouse's | Right: the Mole gives all his savings to the field-mouse with the lantern | The field-mice pay | The Badger pays
test: Is this TRUE or FALSE? «The field-mouse who was asked to recite gave a wonderful speech from the play.» | False: he was too shy and stayed tongue-tied | True: he recited the whole play with great feeling | True, everyone clapped | False: he sang a song instead
test: The field-mice drink mulled ale in this old book. What do we know today? | Alcohol is harmful for children, and they must not drink it | A little warm beer is good for growing children in cold weather | It's fine if it's warm | Field-mice can drink anything
test: «Sweet as, the Rat sorted it,» says Tama from Auckland. (sweet as = great) What was the Rat's quiet aim all evening, as the last part says? | To bring the Mole into a happy, settled frame of mind | To show off how rich and clever he is in front of the guests | To get home fast | To win the field-mice's praise
test: Why does the Rat say little at supper? | It's the Mole's homecoming; he keeps himself in the background | He is too tired and cross to talk to anyone at the table tonight | He doesn't like mice | He has a sore throat
test: Looking back: in chapter IV, the Badger gave the young hedgehogs something before they left. What? | Sixpence each and a pat on the head | A lantern and a basket of eggs for their mother | A bowl of hot porridge to take with them | A warm woollen scarf for each of them
test: Looking back: what did Rat and Mole pay a porter twopence for in chapter II? | To keep a strict eye on Toad in the waiting-room | To carry their heavy bags and the bird-cage to the train | To mend the cart | To find the horse
test: Looking back: in chapter IV, the Badger said there's no peace «except underground». What does the Mole decide now? | Home underground is precious, but he must go back to the world above | He agrees with the Badger: he'll never go above ground again, ever | He hates being underground | He'll move in with Badger
test: Looking back: at the end of chapter IV the Mole saw he was «an animal of tilled field and hedge-row». How does the end of chapter V fit with that? | Both say: keep to your own place, but enjoy the bigger life too | They don't fit: now he wants to live deep in the Wild Wood like the Badger | They don't fit: now he wants the sea | Both say he must live alone
test: Looking back: how did the Mole spend his first night at the Rat's house in chapter I? | Happy in the best bedroom, the river lapping his window | Crying all night because he missed his home so much | Awake all night, frightened | In the boat
test: Looking back: in chapter I, the Mole begged to do one job by himself at the picnic. What? | Unpack the luncheon-basket | Row the boat home all by himself | Make the tea on a little fire | Catch a fish for their supper
test: Ella, a bit salty: «Mole forgot his house all year, so it should be cross with him.» (salty = cross) How does the book say his home welcomes him? | Smilingly, without rancour — with the same simple welcome | Coldly, with dust and silence, as if it had forgotten who he was | It locks him out | With a party
test: What would happen if the Rat had answered «I don't know, we've nothing» when Mole despaired about the guests? | The field-mice would go home cold and hungry, and Mole would be miserable | The field-mice would cook them all a big supper | Nothing | The Badger would come
test: «Slumber gathered him forthwith, as a swathe of barley is folded into the arms of the reaping machine.» What does this picture mean? | The Rat falls asleep instantly and completely | The Rat goes out into the dark fields to cut the barley by hand | The Rat is wrapped in a blanket of straw | The Rat dreams of farms
test: ★★★ The carol has five verses, and each ends with a line about «joy … in the morning». How many lines end with «morning» in the whole carol? | 5 | 1 | 4 | 10
test: ★★★ «His lady-love had gone into a convent.» In the field-mice's play, what made the ending sad? | The hero got home, but the one he loved had gone away for good | The hero never escaped from the galley and rowed there for the rest of his life | The pirate was his brother | The play had no ending
test: ★★★ The Rat called the Mole's home «capital», «compact», «well planned», and now «ripping». What do all four words tell us about the Rat? | He keeps praising the house to make the Mole feel proud of it | He really thinks it is better than his own house and wants to move in | He is very bad at words | He wants to buy it
exam: Identify the means by which provisions were procured for the entertainment of the field-mice. | A purchase made at the shops by a field-mouse, on the Rat's commission | A thorough search of the Mole's cellar and cupboards by the whole party of guests | A gift from the Badger | A loan from the Otter
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Rat's conduct during the supper? | Quiet attention to the wants of every guest | Loquacious recollection of river adventures throughout the meal | Abstention from the table | Sleep
exam: Ascertain the reason for the field-mouse's failure to recite from the play. | Stage-fright | An injury to the throat sustained during the carol-singing | The prohibition of the Rat | Forgetfulness of the text
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Mole's reflections at the close of the chapter. | That he must return to the larger world, yet value his home as an anchorage | That he must forsake the upper world and remain at Mole End permanently | That he should sell his home | That he had no need of any home
